Test Persistence After Reboot in Windows VM
Learning Objectives
By completing this lab, you will:

Implement persistence mechanisms using Windows services and registry modifications
Test persistence effectiveness after system reboot
Understand how malware maintains access through system restarts
Practice defensive analysis techniques for identifying persistent threats
Prerequisites
Basic understanding of Windows operating system concepts
Familiarity with Linux command line operations
Knowledge of virtualization concepts
Understanding of Windows registry structure
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided system is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required software during the lab exercises.

Required Tools Installation
Task 1: Install Virtualization and Windows VM
Subtask 1.1: Install QEMU and Dependencies
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y qemu-kvm qemu-utils virtinst virt-manager libvirt-daemon-system
sudo systemctl enable libvirtd
sudo systemctl start libvirtd
sudo usermod -a -G libvirt $USER
Subtask 1.2: Download Windows 10 Evaluation ISO
cd /tmp
wget -O windows10.iso "https://software-download.microsoft.com/download/pr/19041.508.200927-1902.20h2_release_svc_refresh_CLIENTENTERPRISEEVAL_OEMRET_x64FRE_en-us.iso"
Subtask 1.3: Create Windows VM
# Create disk image
qemu-img create -f qcow2 /tmp/windows10.qcow2 40G

# Start Windows installation
qemu-system-x86_64 \
  -enable-kvm \
  -m 4096 \
  -cpu host \
  -smp 2 \
  -drive file=/tmp/windows10.qcow2,format=qcow2 \
  -cdrom /tmp/windows10.iso \
  -boot d \
  -vnc :1 \
  -netdev user,id=net0 \
  -device rtl8139,netdev=net0
Subtask 1.4: Install VNC Viewer and Connect
sudo apt install -y tigervnc-viewer
vncviewer localhost:5901
Complete the Windows installation through the VNC viewer interface.

Task 2: Implement Persistence Mechanisms
Subtask 2.1: Create Persistence Scripts
Create a batch script that will serve as our persistent payload:

@echo off
echo Persistence Test - %date% %time% >> C:\temp\persistence_log.txt
timeout /t 60 /nobreak > nul
goto :loop
Save this as C:\temp\persistent_payload.bat in the Windows VM.

Subtask 2.2: Registry-Based Persistence
Open Command Prompt as Administrator in Windows VM and execute:

# Create directory for logs
mkdir C:\temp

# Add registry entry for startup persistence
reg add "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" /v "SystemUpdate" /t REG_SZ /d "C:\temp\persistent_payload.bat" /f

# Verify registry entry
reg query "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" /v "SystemUpdate"
Subtask 2.3: Service-Based Persistence
Create a service wrapper script C:\temp\service_wrapper.bat:

@echo off
:loop
echo Service Persistence - %date% %time% >> C:\temp\service_log.txt
timeout /t 120 /nobreak > nul
goto :loop
Install as Windows service:

# Create service using sc command
sc create "WindowsSecurityUpdate" binPath= "C:\temp\service_wrapper.bat" start= auto DisplayName= "Windows Security Update Service"

# Start the service
sc start "WindowsSecurityUpdate"

# Verify service status
sc query "WindowsSecurityUpdate"
Subtask 2.4: Scheduled Task Persistence
# Create scheduled task for persistence
schtasks /create /tn "SystemMaintenance" /tr "C:\temp\persistent_payload.bat" /sc onstart /ru SYSTEM /f

# Verify scheduled task
schtasks /query /tn "SystemMaintenance"
Task 3: Test Persistence After Reboot
Subtask 3.1: Document Pre-Reboot State
Before rebooting, document the current state:

# Check running processes
tasklist | findstr persistent_payload

# Check registry entries
reg query "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run"

# Check services
sc query "WindowsSecurityUpdate"

# Check scheduled tasks
schtasks /query /tn "SystemMaintenance"

# Check log files
type C:\temp\persistence_log.txt
type C:\temp\service_log.txt
Subtask 3.2: Perform System Reboot
Reboot the Windows VM:

shutdown /r /t 0
Wait for the system to restart and reconnect via VNC.

Subtask 3.3: Verify Persistence After Reboot
After reboot, check if persistence mechanisms are active:

# Wait 2 minutes after boot, then check processes
tasklist | findstr cmd
tasklist | findstr persistent

# Verify registry persistence is active
reg query "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" /v "SystemUpdate"

# Check service status
sc query "WindowsSecurityUpdate"

# Verify scheduled task executed
schtasks /query /tn "SystemMaintenance" /v

# Check log files for new entries
type C:\temp\persistence_log.txt
type C:\temp\service_log.txt
Subtask 3.4: Monitor Persistence Activity
# Monitor for 5 minutes and check log growth
timeout /t 300
type C:\temp\persistence_log.txt | find /c "Persistence Test"
type C:\temp\service_log.txt | find /c "Service Persistence"
Task 4: Analysis and Documentation
Subtask 4.1: Document Findings
Create a summary of persistence effectiveness:

echo === Persistence Test Results === > C:\temp\results.txt
echo Registry Persistence: >> C:\temp\results.txt
reg query "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" /v "SystemUpdate" >> C:\temp\results.txt 2>&1

echo Service Persistence: >> C:\temp\results.txt
sc query "WindowsSecurityUpdate" >> C:\temp\results.txt

echo Scheduled Task Persistence: >> C:\temp\results.txt
schtasks /query /tn "SystemMaintenance" >> C:\temp\results.txt

echo Log Entries Count: >> C:\temp\results.txt
type C:\temp\persistence_log.txt | find /c "Persistence Test" >> C:\temp\results.txt
type C:\temp\service_log.txt | find /c "Service Persistence" >> C:\temp\results.txt

type C:\temp\results.txt
Subtask 4.2: Clean Up Persistence Mechanisms
# Remove registry entry
reg delete "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" /v "SystemUpdate" /f

# Stop and delete service
sc stop "WindowsSecurityUpdate"
sc delete "WindowsSecurityUpdate"

# Delete scheduled task
schtasks /delete /tn "SystemMaintenance" /f

# Remove files
del C:\temp\persistent_payload.bat
del C:\temp\service_wrapper.bat
del C:\temp\persistence_log.txt
del C:\temp\service_log.txt
